# =============================================================================
#  HERITAGE BITES — who gets told when an order comes in
# =============================================================================
#
#  Fill this in once, on the machine or host that runs the site.
#  Everything here is free. No card, no monthly fee, no app to buy.
#
#  WHAT YOU FILL IN, AND WHAT IT BUYS YOU
#
#    PART 1  Your email account        -> you (the kitchen) get an email per order
#    PART 2  Your shop's web address   -> the link inside the customer's receipt works
#    PART 3  Push keys                 -> your phone buzzes the second an order lands
#    PART 4  Optional extras           -> customer receipts that dodge spam
#
#  AFTER FILLING IT IN:
#
#      1. Save this file as  .env      (same folder as package.json)
#         If a .env already exists, COPY THESE LINES INTO IT instead of
#         replacing it — it may already hold your Yoco payment settings.
#      2. npm run check:notifications  -> it logs in, sends you a test and tells
#                                          you line by line what is still wrong
#      3. Restart the site, then open /alerts on the kitchen phone and enrol it
#
#  A value with a space or a # in it must be wrapped in "double quotes".
#  Do not put "export" in front of anything. Do not add a trailing space.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PART 1 — YOUR EMAIL ACCOUNT (this is what sends the kitchen ticket)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  The example below uses Gmail, because the shop already has a Gmail address and
#  it costs nothing. Any email host works — just change the four settings.
#
#  GMAIL, STEP BY STEP (a normal password will NOT work):
#    1. Sign in as heritagebitesfood@gmail.com
#    2. Google Account -> Security -> turn ON "2-Step Verification"
#    3. Still under Security -> "App passwords" (search for it if hidden)
#    4. Name it "Heritage Bites website" -> Create
#    5. Google shows 16 letters in four groups. COPY THEM, DROP THE SPACES.
#       (The site tolerates spaces, but pasting them clean removes a whole class
#        of "why does it not send" problems.)
#    6. Paste the 16 letters into HB_SMTP_PASS below, between the quotes.
#
#  If the Gmail account password is ever changed, Google cancels App Passwords
#  and this stops working. That is the single most common cause of silence.

HB_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
HB_SMTP_PORT=465
HB_SMTP_SECURE=true
HB_SMTP_USER=heritagebitesfood@gmail.com
HB_SMTP_PASS="paste the 16-character Google App Password here"
HB_SMTP_FROM=heritagebitesfood@gmail.com

#  Where the kitchen ticket is sent. This is the inbox somebody actually watches
#  on a Thursday morning. It can be the same Gmail account, or a second one.
HB_ORDER_ALERT_EMAIL=heritagebitesfood@gmail.com

#  Leave as true. "false" switches off every order email and queues them.
HB_ORDER_EMAIL_ENABLED=true


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PART 2 — THE ADDRESS OF THIS WEBSITE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  The customer's receipt contains a private link to their own order page, built
#  from this value. Put the address you would type in a browser to reach the shop.
#
#  *** DO NOT LEAVE THIS BLANK. ***
#  If it is blank the site falls back to https://heritagebites.co.za — which is a
#  DIFFERENT restaurant in Pretoria. A customer clicking the link in their receipt
#  would land on somebody else's website. The site warns you at startup and
#  `npm run check:notifications` refuses to pass while this is missing.
#
#  Rules: include https://, no path, no trailing slash.
#  While testing on your own laptop, http://localhost:4000 is correct.

HB_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-shop-address-here.co.za


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PART 3 — THE PHONE BUZZER (Web Push)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  Email can sit unread. This makes the kitchen phone or tablet buzz within a
#  second of an order, even with the website closed. It is free and unlimited:
#  the browser vendor delivers it, not a messaging company.
#
#  You do not type these by hand. Run, once, in the project folder:
#
#        npm run push:keys
#
#  It prints four lines. Paste the first three below exactly as printed; the
#  fourth (HB_OWNER_PUSH_KEY) is your password for enrolling a phone — keep it
#  long, keep it private, and do not put it anywhere public.
#
#  Then restart the site, open  https://your-site/alerts  on the kitchen phone,
#  paste HB_OWNER_PUSH_KEY, and press the button. Repeat on a second device if you
#  want a backup (a counter tablet, a second phone).
#
#  IMPORTANT:
#    - On an iPhone, Safari only delivers push after the site is added to the
#      Home Screen: Share -> Add to Home Screen (iOS 16.4 or newer). Android and
#      desktop work immediately.
#    - Rotating these keys later means every device must enrol again.

HB_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
HB_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
HB_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:heritagebitesfood@gmail.com
HB_OWNER_PUSH_KEY=

#  Leave as true. Setting it to "false" turns push off.
HB_PUSH_ENABLED=true

#  Must stay false on the live site: "true" means pushes are written to the log
#  instead of being delivered, which looks like it works and alerts nobody.
HB_PUSH_DRY_RUN=false


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PART 4 — OPTIONAL, BUT WORTH IT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#  (a) Customer receipts. These are ON by default and go out from the account
#      above, to whatever email the customer typed at checkout. Nothing to do.
#      But: mail sent "from" a Gmail address whose links point at your shop's
#      domain is what spam looks like, so some receipts will be filtered.
#
#      The fix is a free transactional provider with your domain verified
#      (Brevo's free plan: 300 emails a day, no card). When you have those
#      credentials, uncomment and fill these in. Receipts then use this account
#      and the kitchen tickets keep using Gmail above.
#
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_HOST=smtp-relay.brevo.com
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_PORT=587
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_SECURE=false
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_USER=
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_PASS=
# HB_CUSTOMER_SMTP_FROM=Heritage Bites <orders@your-shop-address-here.co.za>

#  Set to false only if you want to stop receipt emails while keeping the
#  kitchen ticket.
HB_CUSTOMER_EMAIL_ENABLED=true

#  (b) How often the site retries unsent notifications, in milliseconds.
#      5000 = five seconds. Smaller means the kitchen hears sooner; below 1000
#      is ignored.
HB_ORDER_EMAIL_POLL_MS=5000

#  (c) Contact form and "ask us to cook it" enquiries. These go to your Formspree
#      inbox, which is already configured (formspree.io/f/xvkkkwve). Only set this
#      to move them somewhere else, or to an empty value to keep them on the site
#      only:
# HB_CONTACT_FORMSPREE_URL=https://formspree.io/f/xvkkkwve


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  CHECKING IT — do this before the next service window
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#        npm run check:notifications
#
#  It logs in to your mail account for real, sends a test ticket to
#  HB_ORDER_ALERT_EMAIL, sends a test receipt if you pass an address, and lists
#  anything still missing. It exits non-zero, so a monitor can watch it:
#
#        npm run check:notifications -- --to you@example.com
#        HB_BASE=https://your-site npm run notify:check   # the live site's own view
#
#  THE THING TO UNDERSTAND: if any of this is wrong, nothing crashes. Orders are
#  still saved on the site, and the kitchen simply never hears about them. That is
#  why the check exists, run it.
